WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.790
Of all the cosmic heroes roaming around the
spaceways of the Marvel Universe, none have

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:07.680
ever been as prominent as the Silver Surfer.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.530
Created by Jack Kirby in the pages of Fantastic
Four, he made his first appearance as the

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:15.170
herald of the world-devouring Galactus, and
would grow to become one of the biggest cult

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:17.240
favorites on Marvel’s roster.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.960
From his unlikely origins to his his surprising
prominence in cartoons and even video games,

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:26.220
here's the real truth behind Norrin Radd,
the Silver Surfer.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.060
The Coming of Galactus

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:33.750
In 1966, Stan Lee and Jack Kirby plotted a
story for Fantastic Four in which an unstoppable

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.500
cosmic threat showed up to devour the world.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.320
When Kirby turned in the pages for Fantastic
Four #48, though, Stan was surprised to find

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.980
that they featured a chrome-plated spaceman
flying around on a surfboard.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:49.520
Kirby explained that Galactus was such a big
deal that he needed a herald to announce his

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.370
presence, and since surfing was all over pop
culture in the ‘60s, from the Beach Boys

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.910
to Batman, his new character wound up on a
cosmic surfboard.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.489
"Surfing's not a sport, it's a way of life!"

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:03.350
Lee was initially wary of adding something
so weird into the middle of a high-stakes

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:05.080
superhero drama, but it worked.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:08.970
In a time when most of Marvel’s competitors
were still filling every issue with two or

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:13.700
three stories, “The Coming of Galactus”
kicked off a modern, multi-part epic, with

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:16.670
its title villain only showing up on the last
page.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:20.860
The rest of the issue was devoted to the Watcher
and the Silver Surfer, assuring readers that

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:26.030
if characters this powerful were the opening
act, Galactus must be a truly big deal.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:27.850
The Silver Symbolism

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:31.700
The initial fan reaction to the Surfer was
more than a little divisive.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:37.180
As the letters rolled into the Marvel offices
in 1966, some readers commented that "Silver

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:41.689
Surfer is a corny name," while one wrote in
with an angry screed asking "Why?

00:01:41.689 --> 00:01:45.880
Why don't you give the Fantastic Four a person,
a single person, to fight, and not a bunch

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:50.440
of space bums running around cluttering up
the yarn and making the pages look utterly

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:51.440
disgraceful?"

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:52.440
Ouch.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:56.600
Others were a little more kind, but the strangest
commentary came from a Houston, Texas reader

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:57.790
named Greg Jones

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:03.469
"Galactus represented the Viet Cong, the powerful
aggressor, but not realizing fully his error.

00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:07.471
[...] The Silver Surfer, as America, helped
a lesser people in their time of need, even

00:02:07.471 --> 00:02:09.619
though he himself was not involved.

00:02:09.619 --> 00:02:14.010
And Alicia characterized Stan, Marvel, the
President, and all other loyal, patriotic

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:15.239
Americans."

00:02:15.239 --> 00:02:19.519
Needless to say, that's a pretty wild interpretation
of the a story about a space giant with a

00:02:19.519 --> 00:02:21.659
big hat trying to eat the planet.

00:02:21.659 --> 00:02:25.660
Even Stan Lee seemed surprised by this one,
writing "We can't dot an i without someone

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:27.769
reading some deep subliminal message into
it.”

00:02:27.769 --> 00:02:30.579
​The Surfer goes solo

00:02:30.579 --> 00:02:34.420
Despite his initial concerns, Lee clearly
took a liking to the Surfer by the end of

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:38.989
that first story, likely because he allowed
Stan to explore the kind of sci-fi storytelling

00:02:38.989 --> 00:02:41.559
that he’d pursued before getting into comics.

00:02:41.559 --> 00:02:45.540
Joined by artist John Buscema, Lee launched
a series that would detail the Surfer’s

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:46.540
exile on Earth.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:50.700
They revealed that he was Norrin Radd, a would-be
victim of Galactus’s hunger who gave up

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.169
his humanity to become a herald for the Devourer
of Worlds.

00:02:54.169 --> 00:02:58.129
It represents some of Stan's best work of
the era, with more philosophical stories than

00:02:58.129 --> 00:03:00.900
the other action-packed tales he was scripting
at the time.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:06.790
"I made him a philosophical observer of the
world and the universe."

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:08.930
There were, however, a few problems.

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:11.180
For one thing, the book just didn’t sell.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.689
Despite Stan's personal investment in the
series and the Surfer’s status as a cult

00:03:14.689 --> 00:03:18.930
favorite, that original series ended after
only 18 issues.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:22.900
Even worse, it deepend the rift between Lee
and the Surfer’s creator, Jack Kirby, who

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:26.239
had a completely different idea for what kind
of stories to tell.

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:30.400
Lee attempted to make amends by bringing Kirby
in to tell the story of a more savage Surfer

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.900
who would act as cosmic power personified,
but that story never happened, it was announced

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.689
to come next month in that final issue of
the series.

00:03:37.689 --> 00:03:42.189
Later that year, Kirby would leave Marvel
for DC and the promise of more creative control,

00:03:42.189 --> 00:03:46.169
summing up his reasons with the simple statement
"I'm not going to give them another Silver

00:03:46.169 --> 00:03:47.169
Surfer.”

00:03:47.169 --> 00:03:49.219
Now you’re playing with the Power Cosmic

00:03:49.219 --> 00:03:52.959
Given Stan Lee’s affection for the Surfer,
it’s no surprise that Marvel would get behind

00:03:52.959 --> 00:03:55.719
more than a few attempts to bring him to mass
media.

00:03:55.719 --> 00:04:00.709
There was a well-received but short-lived
cartoon in 1998, but in 1990, the Surfer made

00:04:00.709 --> 00:04:03.809
his video game debut on the Nintendo Entertainment
System.

00:04:03.809 --> 00:04:08.409
To give you an idea of where he ranked, the
only other Marvel games on the NES at the

00:04:08.409 --> 00:04:12.680
time starred the X-Men and the Punisher, two
of the most popular franchises of the era.

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:16.840
He even managed to beat Spider-Man to his
NES debut by two full years.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:24.640
(the only thing worse than comic book-based NES games were their commercials)

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:30.020
Unfortunately, being early doesn't always
mean being great.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:34.129
While it boasted some pretty incredible graphics
and a killer soundtrack, Silver Surfer is

00:04:34.129 --> 00:04:38.250
mostly remembered for a staggering difficulty
level that kept almost everyone who played

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:40.360
it from actually beating the game.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:43.710
If they had, though, they would've been treated
to a pretty bizarre plot.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:47.860
Dispatched on a mission of cosmic importance
by Galactus, the Silver Surfer faces foes

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:49.810
like Mephisto and Firelord.

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:54.610
The final boss, though, is Mr. Sinister, an
X-Men foe with no real connection to the cosmic

00:04:54.610 --> 00:05:00.430
side of the Marvel Universe, who is depicted
as a 15 foot-tall purple hulk with a handgun

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:02.600
and a pair of white go-go boots.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:05.469
Maybe it's for the best that nobody ever got
to see him.

00:05:05.469 --> 00:05:09.440
"I shall give birth to the future!"

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:10.440
Okay, weirdo.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:13.280
The cinematic rise (and fall) of the Silver
Surfer

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:19.240
In 1979, after Superman had become a massive
box office success, Hollywood was suddenly

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:21.740
very interested in superhero movies.

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:25.710
In an attempt to get Marvel a foothold in
the film industry, Stan Lee spent a year in

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:29.810
Los Angeles shopping around various projects,
including a Silver Surfer pitch.

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:34.770
It was picked up, too, but despite being assigned
a respectable $25,000,000 budget and having

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:39.830
Olivia Newton-John attached to star, the Surfer
never managed to escape to the big screen.

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:44.810
28 years later, he’d finally make it, thanks
to 2007’s Fantastic Four: Rise of the Silver

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:45.810
Surfer.

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:49.729
As the title implies, there were hints at
plans to spin the skyrider of the spaceways

00:05:49.729 --> 00:05:52.069
off into his own big-screen adventure.

00:05:52.069 --> 00:05:56.449
Unfortunately, that film’s failure at the
box office put an end to that, and to plans

00:05:56.449 --> 00:06:00.969
for a third FF movie that would’ve potentially
involved the Skrulls and the Inhumans showing

00:06:00.969 --> 00:06:02.530
up.

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:06.349
In 2015, Marvel’s first family was rebooted
with another movie that landed with an even

00:06:06.349 --> 00:06:10.749
more resounding thud, managing to perform
even worse than its predecessors.

00:06:10.749 --> 00:06:13.289
"It's clobberin time."

00:06:13.289 --> 00:06:17.099
But with 35 years of Marvel trying to bring
the Surfer to the big screen, it's easy to

00:06:17.099 --> 00:06:21.039
look at Rise of the Silver Surfer as a mere
setback rather than the final nail in the

00:06:21.039 --> 00:06:22.039
coffin.

00:06:22.039 --> 00:06:26.169
The success of cosmic-focused films like Guardians
of the Galaxy and the Surfer's prominence

00:06:26.169 --> 00:06:30.560
in stories like the original Infinity Gauntlet
mean that it’s not too unlikely that we’ll

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:34.120
see Norrin Radd surfing his way into the MCU…
someday.

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:35.840
Dedicated fans are waiting.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:36.840
Thanks for watching!

